#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101945 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. What would the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ look like, and how does it relate to the original curve?\n\n![](images/1a/1a55ebd7e9dc656f3a2488debf14e63e655ed7548b76f63c26498f09d96d65ca.png)\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units greater. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)+2[/math]} \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (0,3.5) \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ is precisely two units above a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)+2$ is an **upward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/66/6636d65acf9cd34d818d3d9e12a7b7de36dec4ceb70c7503a08751f971868855.png)\n\nOn the other hand, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units smaller. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)-2[/math]} \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,-2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (0,-0.5) \\\\ (1,2) & (1,0) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is two units below a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is a **downward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/72/72588aaa92f5b4bf9923c381cf5ad28ebb7288e77f6442549d0ffe1fdac3dc48.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "198f4c8f-65df-5b93-951c-12fd4d9dfd12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2809 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101972 :tutorial/content "Consider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve $y=f(x)+b$ shown below. What is the value of $b?$\n\n![](images/b0/b058da1ea31e45f3029f1542642d802a35da7280f315a37ea92044e5c322fbd4.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+b$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $6$ units down. So, $b=-6.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f2c0aba0-7e3b-5a75-83b0-7386793a015b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3295 :tutorial/title "Identifying the Shift"}]}
